#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064096 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "236f51c2-22ad-551f-afb4-62fd6de6d21e" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Linear Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "4d4edfc8-e178-58b4-b827-e4310ead130e" :question/math-academy-id "e-485" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 \\dfrac{4x-1}{x+1}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "The denominator of the integrand doesn't divide the numerator exactly, so we need to decompose it into its quotient and remainder. For this, we can use synthetic division.\n\nWe start by drawing up our synthetic division table:\n\n|  | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}-1$ | $4$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $4$ | $-5$ |\n\nSo, from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4x-1}{x+1} = 4 -\\dfrac{5}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{4x-1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4-\\frac{5}{x+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4\\,\\text{d}x-5{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4x{|}_{0}^{1}-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[4-0]-5[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1] \\\\  & =4-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064097 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064236 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f110daa3-fd31-5e34-8a88-0e002c2e418a" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Function Whose Denominator is Linear"}] :question/id #uuid "74761123-d539-5d78-8dcd-4ea1db04bed0" :question/math-academy-id "e-486" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{2x^2-3x-4}{x-3}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "We first need to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder parts, which we can do using synthetic division. \n\nWe draw up a synthetic division table:\n\n|  | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}3$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n|  | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n\nSo, from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac{ 2x^2-3x-4}{x-3} = 2x + 3 + \\dfrac {5}{x-3} .\n\t}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2{x}^{2}-3x-4}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+3+\\frac{5}{x-3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+3x+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064098 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064244 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7d71bc2-c0f0-5504-9f79-a5f5cdb8cd92" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Function Whose Denominator Contains a Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "cf016fa7-c3a2-5f9b-84a6-7b005d0d7286" :question/math-academy-id "e-487" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{x-1}{2x-1}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we pull out the factor of $2$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-1}{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{x-1}{2(x-\\frac{1}{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{x-1}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n|  | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}\\dfrac12$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $\\dfrac12$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-\\dfrac12$ |\n\nSo, from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-1}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})} & =1-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2(x-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-1}{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int (1-\\frac{1}{2x-1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[x-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|]+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250317 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064244 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7d71bc2-c0f0-5504-9f79-a5f5cdb8cd92" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Function Whose Denominator Contains a Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a9826cc-221a-58f0-a949-003259d10830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{36}(1+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247516} {:answer/id #uuid "1935d4e4-d30d-5335-ab50-3b3fa04366e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}(1+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247651} {:answer/id #uuid "3c9aeadf-fded-5a2b-861d-58af4c73c465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}(5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247959} {:answer/id #uuid "54ac800d-c455-530d-b963-3aea2be7c145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}(3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248185} {:answer/id #uuid "6b902fe9-3b07-57e2-870c-2a793e643e2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{36}(1+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b902fe9-3b07-57e2-870c-2a793e643e2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{36}(1+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248379} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3226bde-c895-54e8-98cf-b5b2df79f6fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "462b058c-0216-5b9a-aa39-6bb6d7bc768e" :question/math-academy-id "q-114896" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-1/12}^{0}\\frac{2x+1}{6x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we pull out the factor of $6$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1/12}^{0}\\frac{2x+1}{6x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1/12}^{0}\\frac{2x+1}{6(x+\\frac{1}{6})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{-1/12}^{0}\\frac{2x+1}{(x+\\frac{1}{6})}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x & {x}^{0} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6} & 2 & 1 \\\\  & ↓ & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & 2 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+1}{(x+\\frac{1}{6})} & =2+\\frac{(\\frac{2}{3})}{(x+\\frac{1}{6})} \\\\  & =2+\\frac{4}{6x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1/12}^{0}\\frac{2x+1}{6x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{-1/12}^{0}(2+\\frac{4}{6x+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[2{\\int }_{-1/12}^{0}\\text{d}x+4{\\int }_{-1/12}^{0}\\frac{1}{6x+1}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{[2x+\\frac{4}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|6x+1|]}_{-1/12}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{[2x+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|6x+1|]}_{-1/12}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[2(0-(-\\frac{1}{12}))+\\frac{2}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|6(0)+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|6(-\\frac{1}{12})+1|)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[2(\\frac{1}{12})+\\frac{2}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}(1+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320963 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "236f51c2-22ad-551f-afb4-62fd6de6d21e" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Linear Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9eaa259b-afa9-5deb-8159-7c3b66adff9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9eaa259b-afa9-5deb-8159-7c3b66adff9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319866} :answer-field/id #uuid "aec87038-1d77-52ab-aafa-5ef7ab429461" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320515} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adb6b3c7-0cf3-5820-a797-a5dce7bcb453" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "adb6b3c7-0cf3-5820-a797-a5dce7bcb453" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319919} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7dd8807-4f91-542d-a4c2-b9b261609c6d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "420b9f17-222f-5f30-947e-edc02435b92c" :question/math-academy-id "q-352220" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\frac{9x+7}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}} $-\\,29\\operatorname{ln}⁡(${{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The denominator of the integrand doesn't divide the numerator exactly, so we need to decompose it into its quotient and remainder. For this, we can use synthetic division.\n\nWe start by drawing up our synthetic division table:\n\n|  | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $9$ | $7$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-36$ |\n|  | $9$ | $-29$ |\n\nSo, from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\frac{9x+7}{x+4}=9-\\frac{29}{x+4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\frac{9x+7}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}(9-\\frac{29}{x+4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\text{d}x-29{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9x\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}-29\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|\\,{|}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =9(2-(-1))-29(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2+4|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|-1+4|) \\\\  & =27-29(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =27-29\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{6}{3}) \\\\  & =27-29\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\,2\\,).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064865 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064236 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f110daa3-fd31-5e34-8a88-0e002c2e418a" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Function Whose Denominator is Linear"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af1e8d70-28bb-5df4-8fe5-213bde887a0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}x^{2} - x - 4\\ln \\mid x - 4 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063247} {:answer/id #uuid "c13b3960-cb3e-5a6d-ab04-4e7c06ecd9e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} - 6\\ln \\mid x - 4 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063248} {:answer/id #uuid "0acf09a8-8d8d-5512-9461-c204fa18b0fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x^{2} - 17x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063249} {:answer/id #uuid "529e88ef-a319-5d09-85b6-887cb2c3a2aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x^{2} - x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063250} {:answer/id #uuid "60935745-bc2b-58c9-b2a0-9cba70dc76f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} - 4x + \\ln \\mid x - 4 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063251}] :answer-field/id #uuid "943cb8b2-64c1-553e-80f2-f883b3ab8a15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063246}] :question/id #uuid "ea9dfa89-06eb-5760-a821-cda00c20e204" :question/math-academy-id "q-49458" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int\\frac{4x^{2} - 17x + 4}{x - 4}dx$."}]}
